      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How Music Works\nHow does a note on paper become a\nsound, and how does a listener\nhear it? Music is sound arranged\nin time. Its basic building block\nis the note: one distinct sound\nwith a pitch, a length and a\nloudness. The word also names the\nsymbol that writes the sound on\npaper ([Musical note](https://en.w\nikipedia.org/wiki/Musical_note)).\nPitch is how high or low a\nlistener hears a sound.\n\nA note travels along a path. A\ncomposer writes it on a score. A\nplayer reads the score and presses\na key, bows a string or sings. The\ninstrument makes the air vibrate,\nand the vibration reaches the ear\nof a listener, who hears a pitch\nagain. This map follows one note\nalong that path, in five parts:\n\n- [Sound and\nPitch](#sound-and-pitch): the\nsound of one note is a pressure\nwave that repeats. The wave is a\nsum of sine waves at whole\nmultiples of one frequency, and\nthe listener hears that frequency\nas the pitch.\n- [The Pitch\nSystem](#pitch-system): Western\nmusic uses a fixed grid of\npitches. The grid splits each\noctave, which is a doubling of\nfrequency, into 12 equal steps\ncalled semitones.\n- [The Piano\nKeyboard](#piano-keyboard): the\npiano has one key for each of 88\npitches of the grid, from 27.5 Hz\nto 4,186 Hz.\n- [Sheet Music](#sheet-music): a\nscore works like a plot of pitch\nagainst time. The height of a note\non the staff gives its key, and\nthe shape of the note gives its\nlength.\n- [Hearing Pitch](#hearing-pitch):\nthe inner ear splits sound into\nfrequency bands. Many musicians\ncan hear the interval between two\nnotes, but few people can name a\nsingle note.\n\nMusicians also say tone. A musical\ntone is a steady periodic sound\nwith a length, a pitch, a loudness\nand a timbre ([Musical tone](https\n://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Musical_t\none)). The word has two more\nmeanings in music. A whole tone is\na step of two semitones, and tone\ncolor is another name for\n[Timbre](#timbre), the sound\nquality that tells a piano from a\nviolin.",
      "fontSize": 18,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 22.5,
      "containerId": "node-64b454d7810a",
      "originalText": "How Music Works\nHow does a note on paper become a sound, and how does a listener hear it? Music is sound arranged in time. Its basic building block is the note: one distinct sound with a pitch, a length and a loudness. The word also names the symbol that writes the sound on paper ([Musical note](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Musical_note)). Pitch is how high or low a listener hears a sound.\n\nA note travels along a path. A composer writes it on a score. A player reads the score and presses a key, bows a string or sings. The instrument makes the air vibrate, and the vibration reaches the ear of a listener, who hears a pitch again. This map follows one note along that path, in five parts:\n\n- [Sound and Pitch](#sound-and-pitch): the sound of one note is a pressure wave that repeats. The wave is a sum of sine waves at whole multiples of one frequency, and the listener hears that frequency as the pitch.\n- [The Pitch System](#pitch-system): Western music uses a fixed grid of pitches. The grid splits each octave, which is a doubling of frequency, into 12 equal steps called semitones.\n- [The Piano Keyboard](#piano-keyboard): the piano has one key for each of 88 pitches of the grid, from 27.5 Hz to 4,186 Hz.\n- [Sheet Music](#sheet-music): a score works like a plot of pitch against time. The height of a note on the staff gives its key, and the shape of the note gives its length.\n- [Hearing Pitch](#hearing-pitch): the inner ear splits sound into frequency bands. Many musicians can hear the interval between two notes, but few people can name a single note.\n\nMusicians also say tone. A musical tone is a steady periodic sound with a length, a pitch, a loudness and a timbre ([Musical tone](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Musical_tone)). The word has two more meanings in music. A whole tone is a step of two semitones, and tone color is another name for [Timbre](#timbre), the sound quality that tells a piano from a violin.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "how-music-works"
      }
    },
