      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A Note Is a Sum of Harmonics\nThe signal of a sustained note from a string,\na wind instrument or a voice repeats with a\nperiod T. As a periodic signal, it is a\nFourier series: a sum of sine waves at the\nfundamental frequency f0 = 1/T and at the\nwhole multiples 2\u00b7f0, 3\u00b7f0, 4\u00b7f0 and so on.\nEach sine wave has its own amplitude and phase\n([Musical tone](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/\nMusical_tone)). Musicians call these sine\nwaves harmonics, and the list of them is the\n[harmonic series](https://en.wikipedia.org/wik\ni/Harmonic_series_%28music%29). The\nfundamental is the first harmonic. The tuning\nnote of an orchestra, A4, is a combination of\n440 Hz, 880 Hz, 1,320 Hz, 1,760 Hz and higher\nmultiples ([Timbre](https://en.wikipedia.org/w\niki/Timbre)).\n\nA real note is more than this steady part. Its\nstart, the attack, has transients that do not\nrepeat, and its envelope and pitch can change\nwhile it sounds ([Musical tone](https://en.wik\nipedia.org/wiki/Musical_tone)).\n\nThe physics of the instrument makes the\nmultiples. A string is fixed at both ends, so\nit vibrates only in modes that fit a whole\nnumber of half waves along its length. Mode n\nhas the frequency n\u00b7f0, and the string\nvibrates in many modes at the same time. The\nair column of a wind instrument works the same\nway ([harmonic series](https://en.wikipedia.or\ng/wiki/Harmonic_series_%28music%29)). In a\npiano, a bridge passes the vibration of the\nstrings to the soundboard, which couples it to\nthe air ([Piano](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki\n/Piano)).\n\nThree words name the parts of the spectrum. A\npartial is any sine component. A harmonic is a\npartial at a whole multiple of f0. An overtone\nis any partial above the lowest one. A pure\ntone is a single sine wave. A synthesizer can\nmake one, and some flutes and ocarinas come\nclose ([harmonic series](https://en.wikipedia.\norg/wiki/Harmonic_series_%28music%29)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-0080c4122ace",
      "originalText": "A Note Is a Sum of Harmonics\nThe signal of a sustained note from a string, a wind instrument or a voice repeats with a period T. As a periodic signal, it is a Fourier series: a sum of sine waves at the fundamental frequency f0 = 1/T and at the whole multiples 2\u00b7f0, 3\u00b7f0, 4\u00b7f0 and so on. Each sine wave has its own amplitude and phase ([Musical tone](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Musical_tone)). Musicians call these sine waves harmonics, and the list of them is the [harmonic series](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harmonic_series_%28music%29). The fundamental is the first harmonic. The tuning note of an orchestra, A4, is a combination of 440 Hz, 880 Hz, 1,320 Hz, 1,760 Hz and higher multiples ([Timbre](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timbre)).\n\nA real note is more than this steady part. Its start, the attack, has transients that do not repeat, and its envelope and pitch can change while it sounds ([Musical tone](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Musical_tone)).\n\nThe physics of the instrument makes the multiples. A string is fixed at both ends, so it vibrates only in modes that fit a whole number of half waves along its length. Mode n has the frequency n\u00b7f0, and the string vibrates in many modes at the same time. The air column of a wind instrument works the same way ([harmonic series](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harmonic_series_%28music%29)). In a piano, a bridge passes the vibration of the strings to the soundboard, which couples it to the air ([Piano](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano)).\n\nThree words name the parts of the spectrum. A partial is any sine component. A harmonic is a partial at a whole multiple of f0. An overtone is any partial above the lowest one. A pure tone is a single sine wave. A synthesizer can make one, and some flutes and ocarinas come close ([harmonic series](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harmonic_series_%28music%29)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "harmonics"
      }
    },
