      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Pitch Follows the Period\nPitch is a perception of the listener, and\nfrequency is a physical quantity that an\ninstrument measures. The American National\nStandards Institute defines pitch as the\nattribute of sound that lets a listener order\nsounds from low to high ([Pitch](https://en.wi\nkipedia.org/wiki/Pitch_%28music%29)). For a\nperiodic or nearly periodic sound, the pitch\nis very close to the repetition rate 1/T,\nwhich is f0.\n\nThe ear finds the period even when the f0\ncomponent is absent. A sound made of only\n1,000 Hz and 1,200 Hz repeats every 5 ms. A\nlistener can hear a pitch of 200 Hz in it, in\naddition to the two tones ([Pitch](https://en.\nwikipedia.org/wiki/Pitch_%28music%29)). This\neffect is the missing fundamental. The heard\npitch is usually the greatest common divisor\nof the frequencies that are present. A\ntelephone passes little sound below 300 Hz,\nbut a male voice with f0 near 150 Hz keeps its\npitch on the phone. The brain computes the\nfundamental from the overtones ([Missing funda\nmental](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Missing_\nfundamental)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-5bfb7116984d",
      "originalText": "Pitch Follows the Period\nPitch is a perception of the listener, and frequency is a physical quantity that an instrument measures. The American National Standards Institute defines pitch as the attribute of sound that lets a listener order sounds from low to high ([Pitch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pitch_%28music%29)). For a periodic or nearly periodic sound, the pitch is very close to the repetition rate 1/T, which is f0.\n\nThe ear finds the period even when the f0 component is absent. A sound made of only 1,000 Hz and 1,200 Hz repeats every 5 ms. A listener can hear a pitch of 200 Hz in it, in addition to the two tones ([Pitch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pitch_%28music%29)). This effect is the missing fundamental. The heard pitch is usually the greatest common divisor of the frequencies that are present. A telephone passes little sound below 300 Hz, but a male voice with f0 near 150 Hz keeps its pitch on the phone. The brain computes the fundamental from the overtones ([Missing fundamental](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Missing_fundamental)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "pitch"
      }
    },
    {
