    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 4893272316,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-1634e6d78e61",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1312.0,
      "y": 640.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 630.0,
      "seed": 714520,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Timbre Is the Shape of the Spectrum\nTimbre, also called tone color, makes a guitar\nand a piano sound different when they play the\nsame note at the same loudness. The Acoustical\nSociety of America defines it as the attribute\nthat lets a listener judge two sounds as\ndifferent when they have the same loudness and\npitch ([Timbre](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/\nTimbre)). Two physical properties set it:\n\n- The spectrum: the relative amplitudes of the\nharmonics. Every instrument of an orchestra\nplays the A4 tuning note at 440 Hz, 880 Hz,\n1,320 Hz and so on, and each instrument has\nits own balance of these amplitudes ([Timbre](\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timbre)).\n- The envelope: the change of amplitude over\ntime. It is often described in four phases:\nattack, decay, sustain and release (ADSR). A\npiano note has a loud attack that fades fast,\nfollowed by a soft sustain ([Piano acoustics](\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_acoustics)\n).\n\nThe player and the maker also shape the\nspectrum. A hard piano hammer makes loud, very\nhigh harmonics, so piano hammers have a felt\ncover that is soft enough to avoid them ([Pian\no](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano)).\nHelmholtz stated that timbre depends on the\namplitudes of the partials and not on their\nphases. Research since then shows that the ear\nacts as a simple Fourier analyzer only under\nrestricted conditions ([Ohm's acoustic law](ht\ntps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ohm%27s_acoustic_l\naw)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-1634e6d78e61",
      "originalText": "Timbre Is the Shape of the Spectrum\nTimbre, also called tone color, makes a guitar and a piano sound different when they play the same note at the same loudness. The Acoustical Society of America defines it as the attribute that lets a listener judge two sounds as different when they have the same loudness and pitch ([Timbre](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timbre)). Two physical properties set it:\n\n- The spectrum: the relative amplitudes of the harmonics. Every instrument of an orchestra plays the A4 tuning note at 440 Hz, 880 Hz, 1,320 Hz and so on, and each instrument has its own balance of these amplitudes ([Timbre](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timbre)).\n- The envelope: the change of amplitude over time. It is often described in four phases: attack, decay, sustain and release (ADSR). A piano note has a loud attack that fades fast, followed by a soft sustain ([Piano acoustics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_acoustics)).\n\nThe player and the maker also shape the spectrum. A hard piano hammer makes loud, very high harmonics, so piano hammers have a felt cover that is soft enough to avoid them ([Piano](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano)). Helmholtz stated that timbre depends on the amplitudes of the partials and not on their phases. Research since then shows that the ear acts as a simple Fourier analyzer only under restricted conditions ([Ohm's acoustic law](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ohm%27s_acoustic_law)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "timbre"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
