    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 3117118676,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-37dfbd41ef74",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1312.0,
      "y": 900.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 560.0,
      "seed": 773236,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Real Partials Run Sharp\nThe whole-number rule is exact for an ideal\nstring, which bends with no resistance. A real\npiano string is stiff. Stiffness makes the\nstring dispersive: the wave speed rises with\nfrequency, so the upper partials sit a little\nabove n\u00b7f0. This deviation is inharmonicity ([\nInharmonicity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I\nnharmonicity)). In a piano, it grows toward\nboth ends of the keyboard. A tuner sets each\nhigher note so that its fundamental matches a\nsharp overtone of a lower note. As a result,\nthe octaves of a tuned piano are slightly\nwider than 2:1. The Railsback curve shows how\nfar each note sits from the grid formula, in\ncents ([Piano acoustics](https://en.wikipedia.\norg/wiki/Piano_acoustics)).\n\nA bowed string, or an air column driven by a\nreed or by the lips, locks its partials to\nexact whole multiples. This effect is\nmode-locking. A struck or plucked string\nvibrates freely and keeps its inharmonicity ([\nInharmonicity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I\nnharmonicity)). Tubular bells, timpani and\nmarimbas have mostly inharmonic partials, but\na few strong partials still give the ear a\npitch. Cymbals make spectra rich in inharmonic\npartials, and they can give no sense of a\nparticular pitch ([harmonic series](https://en\n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harmonic_series_%28music%2\n9)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-37dfbd41ef74",
      "originalText": "Real Partials Run Sharp\nThe whole-number rule is exact for an ideal string, which bends with no resistance. A real piano string is stiff. Stiffness makes the string dispersive: the wave speed rises with frequency, so the upper partials sit a little above n\u00b7f0. This deviation is inharmonicity ([Inharmonicity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inharmonicity)). In a piano, it grows toward both ends of the keyboard. A tuner sets each higher note so that its fundamental matches a sharp overtone of a lower note. As a result, the octaves of a tuned piano are slightly wider than 2:1. The Railsback curve shows how far each note sits from the grid formula, in cents ([Piano acoustics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_acoustics)).\n\nA bowed string, or an air column driven by a reed or by the lips, locks its partials to exact whole multiples. This effect is mode-locking. A struck or plucked string vibrates freely and keeps its inharmonicity ([Inharmonicity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inharmonicity)). Tubular bells, timpani and marimbas have mostly inharmonic partials, but a few strong partials still give the ear a pitch. Cymbals make spectra rich in inharmonic partials, and they can give no sense of a particular pitch ([harmonic series](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harmonic_series_%28music%29)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "inharmonicity"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
