    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 7729320053,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-bb74bc554c05",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 726.0,
      "y": 1550.0,
      "strokeColor": "#0F172A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 377.99999999999994,
      "height": 540.0,
      "seed": 917808,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The Pitch System\nA pitch system is the set of\npitches that a music tradition\nuses. The distance between two\npitches is an interval. Its exact\nsize is the ratio of the two\nfrequencies. Musicians name an\ninterval by counting the letter\nnames that it spans, both ends\nincluded: from B up to D is a\nthird, because B, C and D are\nthree letters ([Interval](https://\nen.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interval_%28\nmusic%29)).\n\nWestern music, the music tradition\nthat grew in Europe, builds its\npitch system in two steps. First,\nit gives notes an octave apart the\nsame name. Next, it splits every\noctave into 12 equal steps. The\nsteps are equal as frequency\nratios, so the grid is uniform on\na logarithmic frequency axis.",
      "fontSize": 18,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 22.5,
      "containerId": "node-bb74bc554c05",
      "originalText": "The Pitch System\nA pitch system is the set of pitches that a music tradition uses. The distance between two pitches is an interval. Its exact size is the ratio of the two frequencies. Musicians name an interval by counting the letter names that it spans, both ends included: from B up to D is a third, because B, C and D are three letters ([Interval](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interval_%28music%29)).\n\nWestern music, the music tradition that grew in Europe, builds its pitch system in two steps. First, it gives notes an octave apart the same name. Next, it splits every octave into 12 equal steps. The steps are equal as frequency ratios, so the grid is uniform on a logarithmic frequency axis.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "pitch-system"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 5375824921,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-eeb9f88010d2",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1300.0,
