    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 3112072901,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-f34b3615a862",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1312.0,
      "y": 1420.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 840.0,
      "seed": 144107,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Twelve Semitones\nWestern music splits each octave into 12 equal\nfrequency ratios. This tuning is twelve-tone\nequal temperament. One step is a semitone,\nwith the ratio 2^(1/12), about 1.059463.\nTwelve semitones multiply to 2, which is one\noctave ([Piano key frequencies](https://en.wik\nipedia.org/wiki/Piano_key_frequencies)). The\nstandard ISO 16 sets A4 to 440 Hz ([A440](http\ns://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A440_%28pitch_standa\nrd%29)). Every other pitch follows from it:\nthe pitch n semitones above A4 has the\nfrequency 440 \u00d7 2^(n/12) Hz, and a negative n\ngives a pitch below A4.\n\nMusicians measure intervals in cents, a\nlogarithmic unit. A semitone is 100 cents and\nan octave is 1,200 cents. The interval from f1\nup to f2 is 1200 \u00d7 log2(f2/f1) cents ([Cent](h\nttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cent_%28music%29)\n).\n\nThe equal steps come close to the whole-number\nratios between neighboring harmonics. The\nfifths and fourths of the grid are almost\nexact, and its thirds are further away ([Equal\ntemperament](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equ\nal_temperament), [Interval](https://en.wikiped\nia.org/wiki/Interval_%28music%29)):\n\n| Ratio | Pure interval | Nearest grid\ninterval |\n|---|---|---|\n| 3:2, harmonic 2 to harmonic 3 | Fifth:\n701.96 cents | 7 semitones: 700 cents |\n| 4:3, harmonic 3 to harmonic 4 | Fourth:\n498.04 cents | 5 semitones: 500 cents |\n| 5:4, harmonic 4 to harmonic 5 | Major third:\n386.31 cents | 4 semitones: 400 cents |\n\nThe 14-cent gap of the major third is large\nenough to hear ([Cent](https://en.wikipedia.or\ng/wiki/Cent_%28music%29)). The harmonics of\none note also land near grid pitches. For A4,\nthe harmonic at 1,320 Hz is 2 cents above E6,\nthe one at 2,200 Hz is 14 cents below C\u266f7, and\nthe one at 3,080 Hz is 31 cents below G7.\nAbout the Numbers shows the calculation.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-f34b3615a862",
      "originalText": "Twelve Semitones\nWestern music splits each octave into 12 equal frequency ratios. This tuning is twelve-tone equal temperament. One step is a semitone, with the ratio 2^(1/12), about 1.059463. Twelve semitones multiply to 2, which is one octave ([Piano key frequencies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_key_frequencies)). The standard ISO 16 sets A4 to 440 Hz ([A440](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A440_%28pitch_standard%29)). Every other pitch follows from it: the pitch n semitones above A4 has the frequency 440 \u00d7 2^(n/12) Hz, and a negative n gives a pitch below A4.\n\nMusicians measure intervals in cents, a logarithmic unit. A semitone is 100 cents and an octave is 1,200 cents. The interval from f1 up to f2 is 1200 \u00d7 log2(f2/f1) cents ([Cent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cent_%28music%29)).\n\nThe equal steps come close to the whole-number ratios between neighboring harmonics. The fifths and fourths of the grid are almost exact, and its thirds are further away ([Equal temperament](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equal_temperament), [Interval](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interval_%28music%29)):\n\n| Ratio | Pure interval | Nearest grid interval |\n|---|---|---|\n| 3:2, harmonic 2 to harmonic 3 | Fifth: 701.96 cents | 7 semitones: 700 cents |\n| 4:3, harmonic 3 to harmonic 4 | Fourth: 498.04 cents | 5 semitones: 500 cents |\n| 5:4, harmonic 4 to harmonic 5 | Major third: 386.31 cents | 4 semitones: 400 cents |\n\nThe 14-cent gap of the major third is large enough to hear ([Cent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cent_%28music%29)). The harmonics of one note also land near grid pitches. For A4, the harmonic at 1,320 Hz is 2 cents above E6, the one at 2,200 Hz is 14 cents below C\u266f7, and the one at 3,080 Hz is 31 cents below G7. About the Numbers shows the calculation.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "twelve-semitones"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 8572181369,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-34a5afb7f5cf",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1300.0,
      "y": 1680.0,
