    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 1100109011,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-34a5afb7f5cf",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1312.0,
      "y": 1680.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 595.0,
      "seed": 49687,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Other Pitch Grids\nOther music traditions use pitches between the\nkeys of the Western grid. An interval smaller\nthan a semitone is a microtone ([Microtonality\n](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microtonality)\n). Four examples:\n\n- Persian classical music lowers a note by\nabout a quarter tone with the koron sign, and\nraises it by a quarter tone with the sori\nsign. A quarter tone is half a semitone, 50\ncents ([Quarter tone](https://en.wikipedia.org\n/wiki/Quarter_tone)). The Iranian musician\nAlinaghi Vaziri set the standard use of the\nkoron sign in the early 20th century ([Koron](\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koron_%28music%2\n9)).\n- Arabic maqam music builds its scales from\nseconds of four sizes. One of them, the\nneutral second, lies between the minor second\nof one semitone and the major second of two\nsemitones ([Arabic maqam](https://en.wikipedia\n.org/wiki/Arabic_maqam)).\n- Indian music theory names 22 shrutis in an\noctave. A shruti is the smallest step of\npitch, and the 7 swaras, the notes of a scale,\nare chosen from them ([Shruti](https://en.wiki\npedia.org/wiki/Shruti_%28music%29)).\n- Indonesian gamelan music uses two tunings:\nslendro, with five notes per octave, and\npelog, with seven. The exact pitches change\nfrom one ensemble to another ([Slendro](https:\n//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slendro),\n[Pelog](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelog)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-34a5afb7f5cf",
      "originalText": "Other Pitch Grids\nOther music traditions use pitches between the keys of the Western grid. An interval smaller than a semitone is a microtone ([Microtonality](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microtonality)). Four examples:\n\n- Persian classical music lowers a note by about a quarter tone with the koron sign, and raises it by a quarter tone with the sori sign. A quarter tone is half a semitone, 50 cents ([Quarter tone](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quarter_tone)). The Iranian musician Alinaghi Vaziri set the standard use of the koron sign in the early 20th century ([Koron](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koron_%28music%29)).\n- Arabic maqam music builds its scales from seconds of four sizes. One of them, the neutral second, lies between the minor second of one semitone and the major second of two semitones ([Arabic maqam](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arabic_maqam)).\n- Indian music theory names 22 shrutis in an octave. A shruti is the smallest step of pitch, and the 7 swaras, the notes of a scale, are chosen from them ([Shruti](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shruti_%28music%29)).\n- Indonesian gamelan music uses two tunings: slendro, with five notes per octave, and pelog, with seven. The exact pitches change from one ensemble to another ([Slendro](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slendro), [Pelog](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelog)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "other-pitch-grids"
      }
    },
