      "locked": false,
      "text": "Instruments and the Grid\nInstruments differ in the pitches that they\ncan make. They fall into three kinds:\n\n- Fixed pitch: each key, string, pipe or bar\nsounds one set pitch. The piano, the harp and\nthe xylophone are examples, and a slide across\none of them is a run of separate steps ([Gliss\nando](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glissando)\n). The piano covers 27.5 Hz to 4,186 Hz. A\nlarge pipe organ goes lower: some organs have\na pipe for C\u22121, which is 8 Hz and below the\nrange of hearing ([Range](https://en.wikipedia\n.org/wiki/Range_%28music%29)).\n- Continuous pitch: the player can set the\nfrequency anywhere in a range. Examples are\nthe violin, viola, cello and double bass,\nwhich have no frets, the trombone, the\ntheremin and the human voice ([Glissando](http\ns://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glissando)). The\ntheremin is an electronic instrument. Its tone\nis the difference frequency of two\nradio-frequency oscillators, and the player\ndetunes one of them with a hand near an\nantenna ([Theremin](https://en.wikipedia.org/w\niki/Theremin)).\n- No pitch: unpitched percussion, such as the\nsnare drum, makes noise-like or inharmonic\nsound with no clear fundamental. It keeps the\nrhythm and adds accents ([Unpitched percussion\ninstrument](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unpi\ntched_percussion_instrument)).\n\nPlayers of continuous-pitch instruments use\npitches between the grid steps. Vibrato is a\nperiodic frequency modulation of a note. A\nsolo singer's vibrato usually swings less than\n100 cents to each side of the note ([Vibrato](\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vibrato)). These\nplayers can also tune an interval to an exact\nwhole-number ratio, such as 5:4 for a major\nthird. Such pure intervals are just intonation\n([Just intonation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wi\nki/Just_intonation)).\n\nThe grid holds 120 equal-tempered notes from\n16 Hz to 16,000 Hz, and a listener can tell\nabout 1,400 pitch steps apart over the range\nof hearing ([Pitch](https://en.wikipedia.org/w\niki/Pitch_%28music%29)). The range of an\ninstrument is the span from its lowest to its\nhighest pitch ([Range](https://en.wikipedia.or\ng/wiki/Range_%28music%29)). The concert flute,\nfor example, has a higher and narrower range\nthan the piano, and flute players call C4 \"low\nC\" ([C (musical note)](https://en.wikipedia.or\ng/wiki/C_%28musical_note%29)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-c22d239322fe",
      "originalText": "Instruments and the Grid\nInstruments differ in the pitches that they can make. They fall into three kinds:\n\n- Fixed pitch: each key, string, pipe or bar sounds one set pitch. The piano, the harp and the xylophone are examples, and a slide across one of them is a run of separate steps ([Glissando](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glissando)). The piano covers 27.5 Hz to 4,186 Hz. A large pipe organ goes lower: some organs have a pipe for C\u22121, which is 8 Hz and below the range of hearing ([Range](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Range_%28music%29)).\n- Continuous pitch: the player can set the frequency anywhere in a range. Examples are the violin, viola, cello and double bass, which have no frets, the trombone, the theremin and the human voice ([Glissando](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glissando)). The theremin is an electronic instrument. Its tone is the difference frequency of two radio-frequency oscillators, and the player detunes one of them with a hand near an antenna ([Theremin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theremin)).\n- No pitch: unpitched percussion, such as the snare drum, makes noise-like or inharmonic sound with no clear fundamental. It keeps the rhythm and adds accents ([Unpitched percussion instrument](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unpitched_percussion_instrument)).\n\nPlayers of continuous-pitch instruments use pitches between the grid steps. Vibrato is a periodic frequency modulation of a note. A solo singer's vibrato usually swings less than 100 cents to each side of the note ([Vibrato](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vibrato)). These players can also tune an interval to an exact whole-number ratio, such as 5:4 for a major third. Such pure intervals are just intonation ([Just intonation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Just_intonation)).\n\nThe grid holds 120 equal-tempered notes from 16 Hz to 16,000 Hz, and a listener can tell about 1,400 pitch steps apart over the range of hearing ([Pitch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pitch_%28music%29)). The range of an instrument is the span from its lowest to its highest pitch ([Range](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Range_%28music%29)). The concert flute, for example, has a higher and narrower range than the piano, and flute players call C4 \"low C\" ([C (musical note)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_%28musical_note%29)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "instruments-and-the-grid"
      }
    },
