    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 1377268596,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-1eac4d5270a9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1312.0,
      "y": 2202.9411764705883,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 525.0,
      "seed": 587840,
      "groupIds": [
        "5ixylus2"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Key Names\nThe keyboard repeats one pattern of 12 keys in\nevery octave: 7 white keys and 5 black keys.\nThe black keys sit in groups of two and three\n([Musical keyboard](https://en.wikipedia.org/w\niki/Musical_keyboard)). The picture shows the\noctave from middle C, C4, up to C5, with the\nname and the frequency of each key.\n\n\n\nThe white keys have the letter names C, D, E,\nF, G, A and B. C is the white key just left of\neach group of two black keys. A black key\ntakes its name from a white neighbor. A sharp\n(\u266f) means one semitone higher and a flat (\u266d)\nmeans one semitone lower, so the black key\nbetween C and D is both C\u266f and D\u266d. Two names\nfor one key are enharmonic names ([Enharmonic \nequivalence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enh\narmonic_equivalence)).\n\nMost neighboring white keys are two semitones\napart, which is a whole tone. E and F have no\nblack key between them, and neither have B and\nC, so these pairs are one semitone apart. Each\nstep to the next key multiplies the frequency\nby 1.059463. From C4 at 261.63 Hz, the twelfth\nkey up is C5 at 523.25 Hz, twice the\nfrequency.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-1eac4d5270a9",
      "originalText": "Key Names\nThe keyboard repeats one pattern of 12 keys in every octave: 7 white keys and 5 black keys. The black keys sit in groups of two and three ([Musical keyboard](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Musical_keyboard)). The picture shows the octave from middle C, C4, up to C5, with the name and the frequency of each key.\n\n\n\nThe white keys have the letter names C, D, E, F, G, A and B. C is the white key just left of each group of two black keys. A black key takes its name from a white neighbor. A sharp (\u266f) means one semitone higher and a flat (\u266d) means one semitone lower, so the black key between C and D is both C\u266f and D\u266d. Two names for one key are enharmonic names ([Enharmonic equivalence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enharmonic_equivalence)).\n\nMost neighboring white keys are two semitones apart, which is a whole tone. E and F have no black key between them, and neither have B and C, so these pairs are one semitone apart. Each step to the next key multiplies the frequency by 1.059463. From C4 at 261.63 Hz, the twelfth key up is C5 at 523.25 Hz, twice the frequency.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "key-names"
      }
    },
