    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 2937735879,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-eadeb4ef2d27",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1312.0,
      "y": 2465.8823529411766,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 525.0,
      "seed": 655306,
      "groupIds": [
        "4rv5c9o5"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Octave Numbers\nA number after the letter gives the octave. In\nscientific pitch notation, the number goes up\nby one at each C, so B3 is followed by C4\n([Scientific pitch notation](https://en.wikipe\ndia.org/wiki/Scientific_pitch_notation)). The\n88 keys run from A0 at the left to C8 at the\nright. The picture shows the whole keyboard\nwith every C named. The shading changes at\neach C, where a new octave number starts.\n\n\n\n| Key | Name | Key number | Frequency |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lowest key | A0 | 1 | 27.50 Hz |\n| Middle C | C4 | 40 | 261.63 Hz |\n| Tuning note | A4 | 49 | 440.00 Hz |\n| Highest key | C8 | 88 | 4,186.01 Hz |\n\nMiddle C is the fourth C from the left ([C\n(musical note)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/\nC_%28musical_note%29)). Key number n has the\nfrequency 440 \u00d7 2^((n \u2212 49)/12) Hz ([Piano key\nfrequencies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pia\nno_key_frequencies)). MIDI, the protocol that\nelectronic instruments use to send notes,\nnumbers the same pitches differently: middle C\nis MIDI note 60 and A4 is note 69\n([MIDI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MIDI)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-eadeb4ef2d27",
      "originalText": "Octave Numbers\nA number after the letter gives the octave. In scientific pitch notation, the number goes up by one at each C, so B3 is followed by C4 ([Scientific pitch notation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scientific_pitch_notation)). The 88 keys run from A0 at the left to C8 at the right. The picture shows the whole keyboard with every C named. The shading changes at each C, where a new octave number starts.\n\n\n\n| Key | Name | Key number | Frequency |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lowest key | A0 | 1 | 27.50 Hz |\n| Middle C | C4 | 40 | 261.63 Hz |\n| Tuning note | A4 | 49 | 440.00 Hz |\n| Highest key | C8 | 88 | 4,186.01 Hz |\n\nMiddle C is the fourth C from the left ([C (musical note)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_%28musical_note%29)). Key number n has the frequency 440 \u00d7 2^((n \u2212 49)/12) Hz ([Piano key frequencies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_key_frequencies)). MIDI, the protocol that electronic instruments use to send notes, numbers the same pitches differently: middle C is MIDI note 60 and A4 is note 69 ([MIDI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MIDI)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "octave-numbers"
      }
    },
