    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 1049788682,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-83bea2c54ae9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1312.0,
      "y": 2725.8823529411766,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 472.5,
      "seed": 99596,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What a Key Press Does\nPressing a key makes a felt-covered hammer\nstrike the strings of that note and fall back\nat once. A bass note has one string, a tenor\nnote two and a treble note three. A bridge\npasses the vibration to the soundboard, which\ncouples it to the air. A harder press makes\nthe hammer hit harder and the note louder.\nWhen the key comes up, a damper stops the\nstrings ([Piano](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki\n/Piano)).\n\nThe pianist therefore controls three things\nfor each note: which key, how hard, and how\nlong the key stays down. These match the\npitch, the loudness and the length that the\nscore asks for. MIDI records a played note in\nthe same terms. A note-on message carries the\nnote number and a velocity, which tells how\nhard the key was struck, and a later note-off\nmessage ends the note\n([MIDI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MIDI)).\n\nThe sustain pedal is the right pedal. It lifts\nall dampers, so the notes keep sounding after\nthe keys come up ([Piano](https://en.wikipedia\n.org/wiki/Piano)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-83bea2c54ae9",
      "originalText": "What a Key Press Does\nPressing a key makes a felt-covered hammer strike the strings of that note and fall back at once. A bass note has one string, a tenor note two and a treble note three. A bridge passes the vibration to the soundboard, which couples it to the air. A harder press makes the hammer hit harder and the note louder. When the key comes up, a damper stops the strings ([Piano](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano)).\n\nThe pianist therefore controls three things for each note: which key, how hard, and how long the key stays down. These match the pitch, the loudness and the length that the score asks for. MIDI records a played note in the same terms. A note-on message carries the note number and a velocity, which tells how hard the key was struck, and a later note-off message ends the note ([MIDI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MIDI)).\n\nThe sustain pedal is the right pedal. It lifts all dampers, so the notes keep sounding after the keys come up ([Piano](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "key-press"
      }
    },
