    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 3618692091,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-8e654eaf22d8",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 726.0,
      "y": 3653.344075643948,
      "strokeColor": "#0F172A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 377.99999999999994,
      "height": 922.5,
      "seed": 341647,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Sheet Music\nSheet music, or a score, writes\nmusic as symbols on a staff. The\nstaff works like a graph of pitch\nagainst time: a higher note sits\nhigher, and the notes are played\nfrom left to right. The horizontal\nposition gives the time only\nroughly. The shape of each note\nand the tempo give the exact\ntiming ([Staff](https://en.wikiped\nia.org/wiki/Staff_%28music%29)).\nNotes stacked at the same\nhorizontal place sound together as\na chord, which is the sound of two\nor more notes at the same time ([C\nhord](https://en.wikipedia.org/wik\ni/Chord_%28music%29)).\n\nBesides the pitch, a note on the\npage shows three more things. Its\nnote value gives its length\nrelative to the other notes. Its\ndynamics tell how loud to play it.\nIts articulation tells how to\nshape its attack and its end\n([Musical note](https://en.wikiped\nia.org/wiki/Musical_note)). Rests\nmark silences, and the tempo sets\nthe speed of the beat. The nodes\nbelow explain each part:\n\n- Notes on the Staff: the pitch.\n- Note Lengths and Rests: the\nlength of each note and of each\nsilence.\n- Beat, Bar and Tempo: the speed.\n- Loudness and Touch: the dynamics\nand the articulation.\n- Reading and Playing a Piece: all\nof them together, at the piano.",
      "fontSize": 18,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 22.5,
      "containerId": "node-8e654eaf22d8",
      "originalText": "Sheet Music\nSheet music, or a score, writes music as symbols on a staff. The staff works like a graph of pitch against time: a higher note sits higher, and the notes are played from left to right. The horizontal position gives the time only roughly. The shape of each note and the tempo give the exact timing ([Staff](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staff_%28music%29)). Notes stacked at the same horizontal place sound together as a chord, which is the sound of two or more notes at the same time ([Chord](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chord_%28music%29)).\n\nBesides the pitch, a note on the page shows three more things. Its note value gives its length relative to the other notes. Its dynamics tell how loud to play it. Its articulation tells how to shape its attack and its end ([Musical note](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Musical_note)). Rests mark silences, and the tempo sets the speed of the beat. The nodes below explain each part:\n\n- Notes on the Staff: the pitch.\n- Note Lengths and Rests: the length of each note and of each silence.\n- Beat, Bar and Tempo: the speed.\n- Loudness and Touch: the dynamics and the articulation.\n- Reading and Playing a Piece: all of them together, at the piano.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "sheet-music"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
