    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 3276889786,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-a485eefd3e48",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1312.0,
      "y": 3133.3035898139888,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 997.5,
      "seed": 793841,
      "groupIds": [
        "r6p5bbk1"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Notes on the Staff\nA staff is five horizontal lines with four\nspaces between them. A note head sits on a\nline or in a space. When no sharp or flat\napplies, each step from a line to the next\nspace up is one white key up ([Staff](https://\nen.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staff_%28music%29),\n[Musical keyboard](https://en.wikipedia.org/wi\nki/Musical_keyboard)). A clef at the left end\nof the staff fixes one line to one note, and\nthe other positions follow from that line\n([Clef](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clef)):\n\n- The treble clef \ud834\udd1e, or G clef, puts G4 on the\nsecond line from the bottom. The curl of the\nclef wraps around that line.\n- The bass clef \ud834\udd22, or F clef, puts F3 on the\nfourth line from the bottom. The two dots of\nthe clef sit above and below that line.\n\nThe clef signs grew out of the letters G and\nF, which scribes once wrote on the reference\nline\n([Clef](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clef)).\n\nPiano music uses the grand staff: two staves\njoined by a brace, with a treble staff above\nand a bass staff below. The right hand\nnormally plays the upper staff, and the left\nhand plays the lower staff. Middle C sits\nbetween the two staves on a short extra line,\na ledger line. Ledger lines extend a staff for\nnotes above or below it ([Staff](https://en.wi\nkipedia.org/wiki/Staff_%28music%29), [Ledger l\nine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ledger_line\n)).\n\nThe picture puts each line and space of the\ngrand staff above its white key. Printed music\nleaves a wider gap between the two staves. The\npicture keeps every step the same height, so\nmiddle C sits exactly halfway between the\nstaves.\n\n\n\nLearners often memorize the line and space\nnames with phrases\n([Clef](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clef)):\n\n| Staff | Lines, bottom to top | Spaces,\nbottom to top |\n|---|---|---|\n| Treble | E4 G4 B4 D5 F5: Every Good Boy Does\nFine | F4 A4 C5 E5: FACE |\n| Bass | G2 B2 D3 F3 A3: Good Boys Do Fine\nAlways | A2 C3 E3 G3: All Cows Eat Grass |",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-a485eefd3e48",
      "originalText": "Notes on the Staff\nA staff is five horizontal lines with four spaces between them. A note head sits on a line or in a space. When no sharp or flat applies, each step from a line to the next space up is one white key up ([Staff](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staff_%28music%29), [Musical keyboard](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Musical_keyboard)). A clef at the left end of the staff fixes one line to one note, and the other positions follow from that line ([Clef](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clef)):\n\n- The treble clef \ud834\udd1e, or G clef, puts G4 on the second line from the bottom. The curl of the clef wraps around that line.\n- The bass clef \ud834\udd22, or F clef, puts F3 on the fourth line from the bottom. The two dots of the clef sit above and below that line.\n\nThe clef signs grew out of the letters G and F, which scribes once wrote on the reference line ([Clef](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clef)).\n\nPiano music uses the grand staff: two staves joined by a brace, with a treble staff above and a bass staff below. The right hand normally plays the upper staff, and the left hand plays the lower staff. Middle C sits between the two staves on a short extra line, a ledger line. Ledger lines extend a staff for notes above or below it ([Staff](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staff_%28music%29), [Ledger line](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ledger_line)).\n\nThe picture puts each line and space of the grand staff above its white key. Printed music leaves a wider gap between the two staves. The picture keeps every step the same height, so middle C sits exactly halfway between the staves.\n\n\n\nLearners often memorize the line and space names with phrases ([Clef](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clef)):\n\n| Staff | Lines, bottom to top | Spaces, bottom to top |\n|---|---|---|\n| Treble | E4 G4 B4 D5 F5: Every Good Boy Does Fine | F4 A4 C5 E5: FACE |\n| Bass | G2 B2 D3 F3 A3: Good Boys Do Fine Always | A2 C3 E3 G3: All Cows Eat Grass |",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "notes-on-the-staff"
      }
    },
