    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 4844048403,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-98595a685737",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1910.8,
      "y": 3280.724826686801,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 437.5,
      "seed": 335173,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Sharps, Flats and Key Signature\nAn accidental is a sign in front of a note\nthat moves the note by a semitone ([Accidental\n](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accidental_%28\nmusic%29)):\n\n- A sharp (\u266f) raises the note one semitone:\nplay the next key to the right.\n- A flat (\u266d) lowers the note one semitone:\nplay the next key to the left.\n- A natural (\u266e) cancels a sharp or a flat.\n\nAn accidental also applies to later notes on\nthe same line or space, up to the next bar\nline. A bar line is the vertical line that\nends a bar of music, and Beat, Bar and Tempo\nexplains bars. A key signature is a group of\nsharps or flats right after the clef. It\napplies to every note with that letter, in\nevery octave, until the piece ends or a new\nkey signature appears. For example, a flat in\nthe top space of the treble staff turns every\nE into E\u266d, including E4 on the bottom line\n([Key signature](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki\n/Key_signature)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-98595a685737",
      "originalText": "Sharps, Flats and Key Signature\nAn accidental is a sign in front of a note that moves the note by a semitone ([Accidental](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accidental_%28music%29)):\n\n- A sharp (\u266f) raises the note one semitone: play the next key to the right.\n- A flat (\u266d) lowers the note one semitone: play the next key to the left.\n- A natural (\u266e) cancels a sharp or a flat.\n\nAn accidental also applies to later notes on the same line or space, up to the next bar line. A bar line is the vertical line that ends a bar of music, and Beat, Bar and Tempo explains bars. A key signature is a group of sharps or flats right after the clef. It applies to every note with that letter, in every octave, until the piece ends or a new key signature appears. For example, a flat in the top space of the treble staff turns every E into E\u266d, including E4 on the bottom line ([Key signature](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Key_signature)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "accidentals"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
