    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 5537512754,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-43c222ac8159",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1312.0,
      "y": 3540.7653125167603,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 420.0,
      "seed": 637694,
      "groupIds": [
        "at7md3bz"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Note Lengths and Rests\nThe shape of a note gives its length relative\nto the other notes. The note head, the stem\nand the flags or beams show the value. Each\nvalue lasts half as long as the value before\nit ([Note value](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki\n/Note_value)). Eighth notes and shorter notes\nare usually joined in groups by beams.\nAmerican and British books use different names\nfor the values, as the picture shows.\n\n\n\nA rest is a silence of a set length. Each note\nvalue has a matching rest with its own sign,\nfor example a quarter rest for the silence of\none quarter note ([Rest](https://en.wikipedia.\norg/wiki/Rest_%28music%29)). A dot after a\nnote makes it 1.5 times as long. A tie is a\ncurved line between two notes of the same\npitch. It joins them into one note that lasts\nas long as both together ([Dotted note](https:\n//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dotted_note), [Tie](ht\ntps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tie_%28music%29)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-43c222ac8159",
      "originalText": "Note Lengths and Rests\nThe shape of a note gives its length relative to the other notes. The note head, the stem and the flags or beams show the value. Each value lasts half as long as the value before it ([Note value](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Note_value)). Eighth notes and shorter notes are usually joined in groups by beams. American and British books use different names for the values, as the picture shows.\n\n\n\nA rest is a silence of a set length. Each note value has a matching rest with its own sign, for example a quarter rest for the silence of one quarter note ([Rest](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rest_%28music%29)). A dot after a note makes it 1.5 times as long. A tie is a curved line between two notes of the same pitch. It joins them into one note that lasts as long as both together ([Dotted note](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dotted_note), [Tie](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tie_%28music%29)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "note-lengths"
      }
    },
