      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Loudness and Touch\nDynamics marks give the loudness. They are\nshort forms of Italian words: p, for piano,\nmeans soft, and f, for forte, means loud. The\nscale runs pp, p, mp, mf, f and ff, from very\nsoft to very loud. The marks are relative and\nset no level in decibels. A crescendo asks for\na gradual rise in loudness, and a decrescendo\nasks for a gradual fall. Scores often draw\nthem as hairpins that span several notes: an\nopening < for a crescendo and a closing > for\na decrescendo ([Dynamics](https://en.wikipedia\n.org/wiki/Dynamics_%28music%29)). On the\npiano, a harder press of the key gives a\nlouder note.\n\nArticulation marks tell how a note starts, how\nlong it really sounds and how it joins the\nnext note ([Articulation](https://en.wikipedia\n.org/wiki/Articulation_%28music%29)):\n\n- Staccato: a dot above or below the note asks\nfor a short note with a silence after it ([Sta\nccato](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staccato)\n).\n- Legato: a slur, a curved line over or under\nnotes of different pitches, asks for notes\nwithout gaps between them. A slur looks like a\ntie, but a tie joins notes of the same pitch (\n[Slur](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slur_%28m\nusic%29), [Tie](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/\nTie_%28music%29)).\n- Accent: a small horizontal wedge > on one\nnote asks for a stressed start that then fades\nquickly ([Accent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wik\ni/Accent_%28music%29)). A hairpin is longer\nand spans several notes.\n\nPiano scores also show where to use the\nsustain pedal ([Piano pedals](https://en.wikip\nedia.org/wiki/Piano_pedals)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-0a900f4864d2",
      "originalText": "Loudness and Touch\nDynamics marks give the loudness. They are short forms of Italian words: p, for piano, means soft, and f, for forte, means loud. The scale runs pp, p, mp, mf, f and ff, from very soft to very loud. The marks are relative and set no level in decibels. A crescendo asks for a gradual rise in loudness, and a decrescendo asks for a gradual fall. Scores often draw them as hairpins that span several notes: an opening < for a crescendo and a closing > for a decrescendo ([Dynamics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamics_%28music%29)). On the piano, a harder press of the key gives a louder note.\n\nArticulation marks tell how a note starts, how long it really sounds and how it joins the next note ([Articulation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Articulation_%28music%29)):\n\n- Staccato: a dot above or below the note asks for a short note with a silence after it ([Staccato](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staccato)).\n- Legato: a slur, a curved line over or under notes of different pitches, asks for notes without gaps between them. A slur looks like a tie, but a tie joins notes of the same pitch ([Slur](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slur_%28music%29), [Tie](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tie_%28music%29)).\n- Accent: a small horizontal wedge > on one note asks for a stressed start that then fades quickly ([Accent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accent_%28music%29)). A hairpin is longer and spans several notes.\n\nPiano scores also show where to use the sustain pedal ([Piano pedals](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano_pedals)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "loudness-and-touch"
      }
    },
