      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 665.0,
      "seed": 127294,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Reading and Playing a Piece\nA beginner can play a piece in this order:\n\n1. Find the clefs. The right hand plays the\ntreble staff, and the left hand plays the bass\nstaff.\n2. Read the key signature. Remember which\nletters are sharp or flat in the whole piece.\n3. Read the time signature and the tempo.\nCount one bar of beats aloud at the tempo\nbefore you start.\n4. For each note, find its line or space, name\nit from the clef, and find its key. Apply the\nkey signature and any accidental earlier in\nthe bar.\n5. Press the key on its beat and hold it for\nthe length of the note. At a rest, lift the\nhand. Press stacked notes together.\n6. Add the dynamics and the articulation.\nPress harder for f and softer for p, and\nrelease early for staccato.\n\nNumbers above or below the notes in beginner\nmusic give the fingering. On each hand, the\nthumb is 1, the index finger 2, the middle\nfinger 3, the ring finger 4 and the little\nfinger 5 ([Fingering](https://en.wikipedia.org\n/wiki/Fingering_%28music%29)).\n\nAn example: a bar on the treble staff in 4/4\ntime at \u2669 = 120 holds a quarter note on the\nbottom line, a quarter note on the second\nline, a quarter rest and a quarter note in the\nthird space. The clef names the three notes\nE4, G4 and C5. The right hand presses E4 at\n0.0 s, G4 at 0.5 s, nothing at 1.0 s and C5 at\n1.5 s, and it holds each key for 0.5 s. The\nbar ends at 2.0 s.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-2de264a7b61e",
      "originalText": "Reading and Playing a Piece\nA beginner can play a piece in this order:\n\n1. Find the clefs. The right hand plays the treble staff, and the left hand plays the bass staff.\n2. Read the key signature. Remember which letters are sharp or flat in the whole piece.\n3. Read the time signature and the tempo. Count one bar of beats aloud at the tempo before you start.\n4. For each note, find its line or space, name it from the clef, and find its key. Apply the key signature and any accidental earlier in the bar.\n5. Press the key on its beat and hold it for the length of the note. At a rest, lift the hand. Press stacked notes together.\n6. Add the dynamics and the articulation. Press harder for f and softer for p, and release early for staccato.\n\nNumbers above or below the notes in beginner music give the fingering. On each hand, the thumb is 1, the index finger 2, the middle finger 3, the ring finger 4 and the little finger 5 ([Fingering](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fingering_%28music%29)).\n\nAn example: a bar on the treble staff in 4/4 time at \u2669 = 120 holds a quarter note on the bottom line, a quarter note on the second line, a quarter rest and a quarter note in the third space. The clef names the three notes E4, G4 and C5. The right hand presses E4 at 0.0 s, G4 at 0.5 s, nothing at 1.0 s and C5 at 1.5 s, and it holds each key for 0.5 s. The bar ends at 2.0 s.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "read-and-play"
      }
    },
