    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 2385439729,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-1bafa0eeb5d1",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1312.0,
      "y": 4580.80579834672,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 595.0,
      "seed": 571794,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The Ear Splits Sound by Frequency\nSound reaches the cochlea, a spiral tube in\nthe inner ear that is filled with fluid. The\nbasilar membrane runs along the tube. Each\npart of the membrane, together with the fluid\naround it, acts like a mass-spring resonator.\nNear the entrance of the tube, at the base,\nthe membrane is stiff and light and responds\nto high frequencies. At the far end, the apex,\nit is floppy and heavy and responds to low\nfrequencies. A sine tone therefore makes one\nplace vibrate most. This map from frequency to\nplace is called tonotopy ([Basilar membrane](h\nttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilar_membrane)\n). About 3,500 inner hair cells along the\nmembrane turn its motion into nerve signals\n([Hair cell](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hai\nr_cell)). Hearing research models each place\nas a band-pass filter, the auditory filter,\nand calls its bandwidth the critical band\n([Critical band](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki\n/Critical_band)).\n\nThe ear hears from about 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz,\nand the upper limit falls with age ([Hearing r\nange](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hearing_ra\nnge)). Above 1,000 Hz, a listener notices a\nchange of about 0.6% in a sine tone, which is\nabout 10 cents. Below 500 Hz, the smallest\nchange that a listener notices is about 3 Hz\nfor a sine tone and 1 Hz for a tone with\nharmonics ([Pitch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wi\nki/Pitch_%28music%29)). A semitone, by\ncomparison, is 100 cents.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-1bafa0eeb5d1",
      "originalText": "The Ear Splits Sound by Frequency\nSound reaches the cochlea, a spiral tube in the inner ear that is filled with fluid. The basilar membrane runs along the tube. Each part of the membrane, together with the fluid around it, acts like a mass-spring resonator. Near the entrance of the tube, at the base, the membrane is stiff and light and responds to high frequencies. At the far end, the apex, it is floppy and heavy and responds to low frequencies. A sine tone therefore makes one place vibrate most. This map from frequency to place is called tonotopy ([Basilar membrane](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilar_membrane)). About 3,500 inner hair cells along the membrane turn its motion into nerve signals ([Hair cell](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hair_cell)). Hearing research models each place as a band-pass filter, the auditory filter, and calls its bandwidth the critical band ([Critical band](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Critical_band)).\n\nThe ear hears from about 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz, and the upper limit falls with age ([Hearing range](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hearing_range)). Above 1,000 Hz, a listener notices a change of about 0.6% in a sine tone, which is about 10 cents. Below 500 Hz, the smallest change that a listener notices is about 3 Hz for a sine tone and 1 Hz for a tone with harmonics ([Pitch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pitch_%28music%29)). A semitone, by comparison, is 100 cents.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "ear-filter-bank"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
