    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 4040339948,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-75d3c34ef30e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1312.0,
      "y": 4840.80579834672,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 525.0,
      "seed": 460426,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Relative Pitch\nRelative pitch is the skill of naming or\nsinging a note by its interval from a\nreference note. It is quite common among\nmusicians. With it, a singer can read a melody\nfrom a score and pitch each note from the note\nbefore it, and a player of a fretless\ninstrument can play in tune with others\n([Relative pitch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wik\ni/Relative_pitch)).\n\nPeople also remember the exact pitch of\nrecordings that they know well, even when they\ncannot name the notes:\n\n- Schellenberg and Trehub played 5-second\nexcerpts of familiar TV theme songs to college\nstudents. Each excerpt was at its original\npitch or moved up or down by 1 or 2 semitones,\nand the listeners picked out the original\npitch level. The study concludes that good\npitch memory is widespread among adults with\nno music training ([Schellenberg and Trehub 20\n03](https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-9280.03432)).\n- Levitin asked 46 people to sing two popular\nsongs from memory. Of them, 40% sang the pitch\nof the recording in at least one trial, and\n44% came within two semitones in both trials\n([Levitin\n1994](https://doi.org/10.3758/BF03206733)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-75d3c34ef30e",
      "originalText": "Relative Pitch\nRelative pitch is the skill of naming or singing a note by its interval from a reference note. It is quite common among musicians. With it, a singer can read a melody from a score and pitch each note from the note before it, and a player of a fretless instrument can play in tune with others ([Relative pitch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relative_pitch)).\n\nPeople also remember the exact pitch of recordings that they know well, even when they cannot name the notes:\n\n- Schellenberg and Trehub played 5-second excerpts of familiar TV theme songs to college students. Each excerpt was at its original pitch or moved up or down by 1 or 2 semitones, and the listeners picked out the original pitch level. The study concludes that good pitch memory is widespread among adults with no music training ([Schellenberg and Trehub 2003](https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-9280.03432)).\n- Levitin asked 46 people to sing two popular songs from memory. Of them, 40% sang the pitch of the recording in at least one trial, and 44% came within two semitones in both trials ([Levitin 1994](https://doi.org/10.3758/BF03206733)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "relative-pitch"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 9086351429,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-ce43a5b163b1",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1300.0,
      "y": 5100.80579834672,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "#F8FAFC",
