    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 9688577685,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-ce43a5b163b1",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1312.0,
      "y": 5100.80579834672,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 455.0,
      "seed": 869721,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Absolute Pitch\nAbsolute pitch, often called perfect pitch, is\nthe skill of naming or singing a note with no\nreference note ([Absolute pitch](https://en.wi\nkipedia.org/wiki/Absolute_pitch)). Levitin\nsplits it into two skills: pitch memory, which\nis common, and pitch labeling, which is less\ncommon ([Levitin\n1994](https://doi.org/10.3758/BF03206733)).\n\nHow many people have absolute pitch is not\nknown exactly. A rate of 1 in 10,000 is widely\nreported ([Absolute pitch](https://en.wikipedi\na.org/wiki/Absolute_pitch)). A 2019 review\nfound a rate of at least 4% among music\nstudents ([Carden and Cline 2019](https://doi.\norg/10.1177/0305735619856098)). A test of\nmusic conservatory students in the United\nStates and China found absolute pitch more\noften in students who started music training\nearlier. For each starting age, it was far\nmore common in the Chinese students. They\nspeak Mandarin, a tone language in which the\npitch of a syllable changes the meaning of a\nword ([Deutsch and others\n2006](https://doi.org/10.1121/1.2151799)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-ce43a5b163b1",
      "originalText": "Absolute Pitch\nAbsolute pitch, often called perfect pitch, is the skill of naming or singing a note with no reference note ([Absolute pitch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absolute_pitch)). Levitin splits it into two skills: pitch memory, which is common, and pitch labeling, which is less common ([Levitin 1994](https://doi.org/10.3758/BF03206733)).\n\nHow many people have absolute pitch is not known exactly. A rate of 1 in 10,000 is widely reported ([Absolute pitch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absolute_pitch)). A 2019 review found a rate of at least 4% among music students ([Carden and Cline 2019](https://doi.org/10.1177/0305735619856098)). A test of music conservatory students in the United States and China found absolute pitch more often in students who started music training earlier. For each starting age, it was far more common in the Chinese students. They speak Mandarin, a tone language in which the pitch of a syllable changes the meaning of a word ([Deutsch and others 2006](https://doi.org/10.1121/1.2151799)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "absolute-pitch"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
