    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 2943719238,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-6118abadaf45",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1312.0,
      "y": 5360.80579834672,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 647.5,
      "seed": 674603,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Pitch Compared With Color\nPitch and color share a structure. Hue is an\nattribute of color, such as red or blue.\nChroma is the matching attribute of a pitch,\nand all pitches with one chroma form one pitch\nclass, so C3 and C4 share the chroma C ([Pitch\nclass](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pitch_cla\nss)). Chroma repeats every octave, and pitch\nheight tells which octave a note is in ([Pitch\n](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pitch_%28music\n%29)).\n\nNaming differs. Almost everyone names colors,\nbut few people name pitches. Researchers\ncompare absolute pitch with color naming: most\npeople learn to name blue from the light they\nsee, and people who hear notes together with\ntheir names early in life are more likely to\nname a C ([Absolute pitch](https://en.wikipedi\na.org/wiki/Absolute_pitch)).\n\nThe sense organs differ too:\n\n- The eye has three types of cone cells, so it\nreduces the spectrum of any light to three\nnumbers. Lights with different spectra can\ntherefore look the same. Such lights are\nmetamers ([Metamerism](https://en.wikipedia.or\ng/wiki/Metamerism_%28color%29)).\n- The ear analyzes the spectrum at many places\nalong the basilar membrane. Two tones that are\nfurther apart than a critical band excite\nseparate places, so the ear resolves them. Two\ntones closer than that interact, and the\nlistener hears beats or roughness ([Critical b\nand](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Critical_ba\nnd)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-6118abadaf45",
      "originalText": "Pitch Compared With Color\nPitch and color share a structure. Hue is an attribute of color, such as red or blue. Chroma is the matching attribute of a pitch, and all pitches with one chroma form one pitch class, so C3 and C4 share the chroma C ([Pitch class](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pitch_class)). Chroma repeats every octave, and pitch height tells which octave a note is in ([Pitch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pitch_%28music%29)).\n\nNaming differs. Almost everyone names colors, but few people name pitches. Researchers compare absolute pitch with color naming: most people learn to name blue from the light they see, and people who hear notes together with their names early in life are more likely to name a C ([Absolute pitch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absolute_pitch)).\n\nThe sense organs differ too:\n\n- The eye has three types of cone cells, so it reduces the spectrum of any light to three numbers. Lights with different spectra can therefore look the same. Such lights are metamers ([Metamerism](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metamerism_%28color%29)).\n- The ear analyzes the spectrum at many places along the basilar membrane. Two tones that are further apart than a critical band excite separate places, so the ear resolves them. Two tones closer than that interact, and the listener hears beats or roughness ([Critical band](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Critical_band)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "pitch-and-color"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
