    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 4428718935,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-3920b1c5238c",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 720.0,
      "y": 5620.80579834672,
      "strokeColor": "#57534E",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 397.8,
      "height": 1413.75,
      "seed": 577889,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "About the Numbers\nThe key counts, frequencies, cents and note lengths\nin this map come from the Python script below. It\nran with Python 3.12 in November 2026. It uses\ntwelve-tone equal temperament with A4 at 440 Hz. A\ntuned piano has stretched octaves, so its real\nfrequencies differ from these values, most at the\ntwo ends of the keyboard. The pictures use the same\nvalues.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\nNAMES = [\"A\", \"A\u266f/B\u266d\", \"B\", \"C\", \"C\u266f/D\u266d\", \"D\",\n\"D\u266f/E\u266d\", \"E\", \"F\", \"F\u266f/G\u266d\", \"G\", \"G\u266f/A\u266d\"]\n\n\ndef key_name(n):\n\"\"\"Name of piano key n (1 = A0, 88 = C8).\"\"\"\noctave = (n + 8) // 12\nreturn f\"{NAMES[(n - 1) % 12]}{octave}\"\n\n\ndef key_freq(n):\n\"\"\"Frequency of piano key n in Hz, with key 49 (A4)\nat 440 Hz.\"\"\"\nreturn 440 * 2 ** ((n - 49) / 12)\n\n\ndef cents(ratio):\nreturn 1200 * math.log2(ratio)\n\n\nwhite = [n for n in range(1, 89) if \"\u266f\" not in\nkey_name(n)]\nprint(\"keys:\", 88, \"white:\", len(white), \"black:\",\n88 - len(white))\nfor n in (1, 40, 49, 88):\nprint(f\"key {n} = {key_name(n)} {key_freq(n):.3f}\nHz\")\nfor n in range(40, 53):\nprint(f\"key {n} {key_name(n)} {key_freq(n):.2f}\nHz\")\n\nfor h in range(1, 9):\nf = 440 * h\nn = round(12 * math.log2(f / 440) + 49)\nprint(f\"{h} x 440 = {f} Hz, nearest key\n{key_name(n)} {key_freq(n):.2f} Hz, {cents(f /\nkey_freq(n)):+.1f} cents\")\n\nfor label, ratio, steps in ((\"fifth 3:2\", 3 / 2,\n7), (\"fourth 4:3\", 4 / 3, 5), (\"major third 5:4\", 5\n/ 4, 4)):\nprint(f\"{label}: {cents(ratio):.2f} cents; {steps}\nsemitones = {steps * 100} cents\")\n\nbpm = 120\nfor label, beats in ((\"whole\", 4), (\"half\", 2),\n(\"dotted quarter\", 1.5), (\"quarter\", 1), (\"eighth\",\n0.5)):\nprint(f\"{label}: {beats} beats = {beats * 60 / bpm}\ns\")\n```\n\nSome lines of its output:\n\n- `keys: 88 white: 52 black: 36`\n- `key 40 = C4 261.626 Hz` and `key 88 = C8\n4186.009 Hz`\n- `key 41 C\u266f/D\u266d4 277.18 Hz`\n- `3 x 440 = 1320 Hz, nearest key E6 1318.51 Hz,\n+2.0 cents`\n- `5 x 440 = 2200 Hz, nearest key C\u266f/D\u266d7 2217.46\nHz, -13.7 cents`\n- `7 x 440 = 3080 Hz, nearest key G7 3135.96 Hz,\n-31.2 cents`\n- `fifth 3:2: 701.96 cents; 7 semitones = 700\ncents`\n- `dotted quarter: 1.5 beats = 0.75 s`\n\nThe pictures are D2 diagrams. Their sources are the\n`.d2` files in the folder `music-d2-diagram`, next\nto this map, and the `d2` command renders each one\nto the `.d2.svg` image beside it. The clef signs in\nthe staff picture are characters of a system font,\nApple Symbols on macOS.",
      "fontSize": 13,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 16.25,
      "containerId": "node-3920b1c5238c",
      "originalText": "About the Numbers\nThe key counts, frequencies, cents and note lengths in this map come from the Python script below. It ran with Python 3.12 in November 2026. It uses twelve-tone equal temperament with A4 at 440 Hz. A tuned piano has stretched octaves, so its real frequencies differ from these values, most at the two ends of the keyboard. The pictures use the same values.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\nNAMES = [\"A\", \"A\u266f/B\u266d\", \"B\", \"C\", \"C\u266f/D\u266d\", \"D\", \"D\u266f/E\u266d\", \"E\", \"F\", \"F\u266f/G\u266d\", \"G\", \"G\u266f/A\u266d\"]\n\n\ndef key_name(n):\n    \"\"\"Name of piano key n (1 = A0, 88 = C8).\"\"\"\n    octave = (n + 8) // 12\n    return f\"{NAMES[(n - 1) % 12]}{octave}\"\n\n\ndef key_freq(n):\n    \"\"\"Frequency of piano key n in Hz, with key 49 (A4) at 440 Hz.\"\"\"\n    return 440 * 2 ** ((n - 49) / 12)\n\n\ndef cents(ratio):\n    return 1200 * math.log2(ratio)\n\n\nwhite = [n for n in range(1, 89) if \"\u266f\" not in key_name(n)]\nprint(\"keys:\", 88, \"white:\", len(white), \"black:\", 88 - len(white))\nfor n in (1, 40, 49, 88):\n    print(f\"key {n} = {key_name(n)} {key_freq(n):.3f} Hz\")\nfor n in range(40, 53):\n    print(f\"key {n} {key_name(n)} {key_freq(n):.2f} Hz\")\n\nfor h in range(1, 9):\n    f = 440 * h\n    n = round(12 * math.log2(f / 440) + 49)\n    print(f\"{h} x 440 = {f} Hz, nearest key {key_name(n)} {key_freq(n):.2f} Hz, {cents(f / key_freq(n)):+.1f} cents\")\n\nfor label, ratio, steps in ((\"fifth 3:2\", 3 / 2, 7), (\"fourth 4:3\", 4 / 3, 5), (\"major third 5:4\", 5 / 4, 4)):\n    print(f\"{label}: {cents(ratio):.2f} cents; {steps} semitones = {steps * 100} cents\")\n\nbpm = 120\nfor label, beats in ((\"whole\", 4), (\"half\", 2), (\"dotted quarter\", 1.5), (\"quarter\", 1), (\"eighth\", 0.5)):\n    print(f\"{label}: {beats} beats = {beats * 60 / bpm} s\")\n```\n\nSome lines of its output:\n\n- `keys: 88 white: 52 black: 36`\n- `key 40 = C4 261.626 Hz` and `key 88 = C8 4186.009 Hz`\n- `key 41 C\u266f/D\u266d4 277.18 Hz`\n- `3 x 440 = 1320 Hz, nearest key E6 1318.51 Hz, +2.0 cents`\n- `5 x 440 = 2200 Hz, nearest key C\u266f/D\u266d7 2217.46 Hz, -13.7 cents`\n- `7 x 440 = 3080 Hz, nearest key G7 3135.96 Hz, -31.2 cents`\n- `fifth 3:2: 701.96 cents; 7 semitones = 700 cents`\n- `dotted quarter: 1.5 beats = 0.75 s`\n\nThe pictures are D2 diagrams. Their sources are the `.d2` files in the folder `music-d2-diagram`, next to this map, and the `d2` command renders each one to the `.d2.svg` image beside it. The clef signs in the staff picture are characters of a system font, Apple Symbols on macOS.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "about-the-numbers"
      }
    },
